"""Compact, byte-exact identity for the Phase 11 Python test toolchain.

The mutation runner executes pytest from the project ``.venv`` while the
production and test source live in disposable checkout snapshots.  Package
versions alone are insufficient evidence for that boundary: an installed
module can change without its version changing, and an unrecorded file can be
added below an import package without appearing in wheel ``RECORD`` metadata.

This module therefore hashes every plain, non-bytecode file below a small,
reviewed set of import trees and their exact ``.dist-info`` trees.  Per-file
identities are reduced into one digest per distribution; receipts retain only
those compact digests.  Ambient ``__pycache__`` trees are excluded only when
the caller proves that CPython is using a separate pycache prefix and cannot
write bytecode.

The result is cooperative tamper evidence, not a hostile-code sandbox or a
cryptographic signature.  Source sentinels additionally compare normalized
loaded function, method, nested-function, and class-contained code objects
with freshly compiled sealed source.  Python does not retain a module's
executed top-level code object, so import-time side effects that leave no
reachable callable code remain a stated residual; the startup/file manifests
still bind their source bytes but cannot retroactively prove those effects.
Dataclass, named-tuple, and decorator factories can also synthesize ``<string>``
code at import time.  Such functions are admitted only through a class or
wrapper shape declared by sealed source plus a closure factory from an exact
reviewed byte scope; their generated bytecode is not independently rebuilt.
"""

